<node id="11104858" title="Re^4: The error says the value is uninitialized, but it works anyway" created="2019-08-22 12:18:57" updated="2019-08-22 12:18:57" _doctext_mode="strict"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="830549">haukex</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;As long, as you find a solution, noone will ask from where the solution came.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Well I'd say not quite. It does matter when you're writing a closed-source product and incorporating open-source licensed components, for example. It also matters in academics. In one of the courses in college where I was a TA, the policy way "copying code from the Internet is ok, as long as it's not a significant portion of the assignment, you can explain how the code works, and you cite your sources", and people still forgot to cite their sources...&lt;/p&gt;
</doctext>
